From: mh2lm clock work <clock@mh2lm>
Subject: [PATCH 0246] clock: read the apps RSC a second time, from a channel that cannot write

Nine clock cards read and wrote the GCC block through a provider that owns it.
The RSC is the other half of the same staging plan and has stayed untouched:
SweetRPMh maps the apps RSC's DRV window, its command DB and one TCS page,
installs the INTID37 handler, and drives four rails and the display ARC chain
through it. Every row of C3 so far would be built on top of that owner, so the
first step is not to change what the owner does but to show that a second
service can reach the same block, decode it from the tree rather than from the
owner's private state, and be structurally unable to disturb it.

What C3-01 is. A second IOService - IOQcomRSCChannel - matched by the ADT node
the generator writes, mapping the same three windows the owner maps, reading the
block's identity, the command DB header and the two TCS words of page 0, and
returning NotReady from submit() without touching memory. It is not a vote, not
a handoff and not a second writer. The card exists so that the numbering, the
window ABI and the refusal vocabulary are in place before C3 asks for anything
that writes.

Where the numbers come from. The three windows are RPMH_WINDOWS of the owner
node - apps DRV at 0x18220000 (0x100), apps TCS at 0x18220d00 (0x3000), and the
command DB locator at 0x0c3f000c (8 bytes) - and the command DB itself is not in
the tree at all: word 0 of the locator is its physical address and word 1 its
size, which is why the card can only be audited by reading the locator first.
The identity is three words of the DRV window: id, then the solver word, then
config, from which major, the TCS count and NCPT decode exactly as the owner
decodes them. The TCS enable and status words are at 0 and 4 of page 0.

Ten loads, enumerated. Three DRV words, two locator words, three command DB
header words (version, magic, flags) and the two TCS words. readRegister() is
the only place a load happens and it is bounded by the mapped window, so the
budget is checkable by eye: the ring prints reads=10 and the host test asserts
that every other accessor in the translation unit is a refusal. There is no
store helper here at all - the only function that looks like one answers
Unsupported without touching its arguments - which is what makes writes=0 a
structural claim rather than a promise.

Refusals before the second read. A window the node does not describe, a physical
address that is not the one this firmware ships, a length shorter than the
window, an identity whose major is outside 1..2, the all-ones solver word, a TCS
count outside [8,18] from the 0x3000 window and the 0x2a0 stride, an NCPT
outside 3..16, a locator that does not resolve, a command DB below 0x80000000 or
misaligned or above 1 MiB or shorter than its own 144-byte header, a descriptor
that will not map, and a magic that is not 0x0c0330db: each stops start() with a
row that names the reason and says how many loads it had spent. A version or
flags word that is not the vendor's is not a refusal - it is the WARN the owner
already prints for the same page, because the layout is bounded by the header,
not by the version.

The interrupt is described, never armed. The node carries sweet,rsc-irq-abi
and sweet,rsc-intid and no GIC range at all - the owner keeps the only handler,
the only mask write and the only TCS enable word. The channel's row therefore
says detail=described-not-armed and its TCS row detail=observed-not-owned, and
the audit checks the ordering: the owner's INTID37 line comes first, the
channel's description after it. Two readers of one block are a cross-check, not
a race, because the channel never enables anything.

Matching had to be added in two places. A new IOService subclass does not
instantiate by being listed in files.arm64: it also needs a personality in
gIOKernelConfigTables, and the personality names the node the generator writes
(sweet,rsc-channel) rather than a class list, so an image without the node
instantiates nothing and maps nothing.

The tree. The generator gains --rsc-channel, which appends one node after
display-rpmh: the same three ranges, abi 1, irq-abi 1, intid 37, no children and
no display-resource dependency. Appending it after the owner is deliberate - the
owner starts first and the channel reads a block that is already owned, which is
the whole point - and removing the node restores the previous bytes exactly,
which the host test asserts rather than asserts-by-eye. The twelve audited ADT
derivations are unchanged; the channel tree is a thirteenth pinned digest, and
the clock-only images of the previous round keep their digests and carry no
channel node.

Host tests. test-mh2lm-rsc-channel compiles the shipped translation unit against
the fake IOKit prelude and drives ten rows: the accepted read, the identity
refusals, the geometry refusals, the magic refusal, the version WARN, a submit
that answers NotReady with no store, and a stop that recounts. Because the
prelude satisfies includes the real kernel provides, the test asserts the source
text for the things only the kernel can supply - the read-only map, the absence
of a GIC mapping, the absence of a second handler registration.

Compatibility. Without the node nothing changes: the previous round's trees,
digests and images are untouched, and an image that carries the node differs
from them only by that node. submit() clears the caller's token, counts the
attempt and returns NotReady, so C3-02 and later can be judged against a client
that was always refused.

diff --git a/dxb/xnu/iokit/Kernel/arm/IOQcomRSC.cpp b/dxb/xnu/iokit/Kernel/arm/IOQcomRSC.cpp
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..74d756a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/dxb/xnu/iokit/Kernel/arm/IOQcomRSC.cpp
@@ -0,0 +1,445 @@
+/* SM8150 apps RSC channel: C3-01, one read-only instance.
+ *
+ * Contract of this candidate, in one place:
+ *  - start() maps the three ADT ranges (apps DRV, TCS array, command DB
+ *    locator) through the provider nub and validates every resolved physical
+ *    address and declared length before the first register read. The command
+ *    DB is a fourth window, resolved from the locator and mapped read-only; it
+ *    is not part of the reg ABI and cannot be pointed anywhere the locator does
+ *    not name.
+ *  - Register access is one load helper: bounds checked against the window,
+ *    offset aligned, guarded by the channel lock, with the read barrier this
+ *    SoC requires. This object has no store helper at all, so "writes=0" is a
+ *    property of the file and not of a code path.
+ *  - Nothing here claims the completion line. The GIC is not mapped, no
+ *    interrupt source is registered, no workloop is created and no TCS or
+ *    IRQ-enable word is written: the display owner keeps both the writes and
+ *    the line, and this card only records what it saw.
+ *  - Refusals are specific: ABI property, range count, address, length, DRV
+ *    major/decoder, TCS count, NCPT, command DB address/size/magic, and the
+ *    IRQ description each fail start with their own row. A tree that half
+ *    matches is never read "as far as it goes".
+ *  - The read budget is fixed and small: three DRV words, two locator words,
+ *    three command DB words and the TCS enable/status pair - ten loads. The
+ *    start row states the count, so a ring and this source can be reconciled
+ *    without guessing.
+ */
+#include "IOQcomRSC.h"
+#include <IOKit/IOLib.h>
+#include <libkern/c++/OSData.h>
+#include <string.h>
+#define SWEET_LOG_SINK IOLog
+#include "../../../bsd/dev/sweet_log_level.h"
+
+#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MH2LM)
+
+#define super IOService
+
+OSDefineMetaClassAndStructors(IOQcomRSCChannel,IOService);
+
+/* The ADT reg order is the window ABI, exactly as the display owner declares
+ * it. These are resolved physical addresses (vendor offsets plus the SM8150
+ * RSC base are already applied in the LG DTS) and they are the only addresses
+ * this channel accepts: a node pointing at another RSC is somebody else's
+ * description and must not be read as this one. */
+static const struct { const char *name; uint64_t physical; uint64_t length; }
+rscRanges[kIOQcomRscRangeCount] = {
+	{ "apps-drv",         0x18220000ULL, 0x100ULL  },
+	{ "apps-tcs",         0x18220d00ULL, 0x3000ULL },
+	{ "apps-db-locator",  0x0c3f000cULL, 8ULL      },
+};
+
+/* The two TCS words this card looks at: word 0 is the interrupt-enable mask the
+ * owner writes, word 4 is the status the RSC raises. Both are loads. */
+enum : uint32_t { kIOQcomRscTcsEnable = 0x000, kIOQcomRscTcsStatus = 0x004 };
+
+static const char *
+rscWindowName(unsigned window)
+{
+	switch (window) {
+	case kIOQcomRscWindowDrv: return rscRanges[0].name;
+	case kIOQcomRscWindowTCS: return rscRanges[1].name;
+	case kIOQcomRscWindowLocator: return rscRanges[2].name;
+	case kIOQcomRscWindowDB: return "apps-command-db";
+	default: return "unknown";
+	}
+}
+
+bool
+IOQcomRSCChannel::validateProperties(IOService *provider)
+{
+	/* One opt-in property, versioned. An absent property means the tree did not
+	 * ask for this channel and start is not even reached; a present but wrong
+	 * one is a refusal, never a default. */
+	OSData *abi=OSDynamicCast(OSData,provider?provider->getProperty("sweet,rsc-abi"):NULL);
+	if (!abi||abi->getLength()!=4) {
+		SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=start result=%d detail=bad-rsc-abi length=%u\n",
+		 kIOReturnBadArgument,abi?(unsigned)abi->getLength():0u);
+		return false;
+	}
+	uint32_t value=0;
+	memcpy(&value,abi->getBytesNoCopy(),4);
+	if (value!=kIOQcomRscAbi) {
+		SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=start result=%d detail=bad-rsc-abi value=%#x expected=%#x\n",
+		 kIOReturnBadArgument,value,kIOQcomRscAbi);
+		return false;
+	}
+	/* No other property arms anything here. The irq description is mandatory
+	 * for the same reason the ranges are: a channel that cannot say which line
+	 * completes it is not the apps RSC, and saying it is how the next card
+	 * avoids inventing a second owner. */
+	return describeIRQ(provider);
+}
+
+bool
+IOQcomRSCChannel::describeIRQ(IOService *provider)
+{
+	OSData *abi=OSDynamicCast(OSData,provider?provider->getProperty("sweet,rsc-irq-abi"):NULL);
+	if (!abi||abi->getLength()!=4) {
+		SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=irq result=%d detail=bad-rsc-irq-abi length=%u\n",
+		 kIOReturnBadArgument,abi?(unsigned)abi->getLength():0u);
+		return false;
+	}
+	uint32_t version=0;
+	memcpy(&version,abi->getBytesNoCopy(),4);
+	if (version!=kIOQcomRscIrqAbi) {
+		SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=irq result=%d detail=bad-rsc-irq-abi value=%#x\n",
+		 kIOReturnBadArgument,version);
+		return false;
+	}
+	OSData *intid=OSDynamicCast(OSData,provider?provider->getProperty("sweet,rsc-intid"):NULL);
+	if (!intid||intid->getLength()!=4) {
+		SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=irq result=%d detail=bad-rsc-intid length=%u\n",
+		 kIOReturnBadArgument,intid?(unsigned)intid->getLength():0u);
+		return false;
+	}
+	uint32_t line=0;
+	memcpy(&line,intid->getBytesNoCopy(),4);
+	if (line!=kIOQcomRscIntid) {
+		SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=irq result=%d detail=bad-rsc-intid value=%u expected=%u\n",
+		 kIOReturnBadArgument,line,kIOQcomRscIntid);
+		return false;
+	}
+	fIntid=line;
+	/* Description only. No GIC map, no enable, no route, no handler - the row
+	 * says so in the field a reader would otherwise have to assume. */
+	SWEET_LOG_INFO("SWEET RSC: phase=irq intid=%u detail=described-not-armed\n",fIntid);
+	return true;
+}
+
+bool
+IOQcomRSCChannel::mapWindows(IOService *provider)
+{
+	if (!provider||provider->getResources()!=kIOReturnSuccess) {
+		SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=map result=%d detail=resources-absent\n",kIOReturnNotReady);
+		return false;
+	}
+	for (unsigned i=0;i<kIOQcomRscRangeCount;i++) {
+		Window &w=fWindow[i];
+		w.physical=rscRanges[i].physical;
+		w.length=0;
+		w.base=0;
+		w.map=provider->mapDeviceMemoryWithIndex(i);
+		if (!w.map) {
+			SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=map resource=%s result=%d detail=window-absent\n",
+			 rscRanges[i].name,kIOReturnNotReady);
+			return false;
+		}
+		uint64_t observed=w.map->getPhysicalAddress();
+		uint64_t length=w.map->getLength();
+		if (observed!=rscRanges[i].physical) {
+			SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=map resource=%s result=%d detail=address-mismatch observed=%#llx expected=%#llx\n",
+			 rscRanges[i].name,kIOReturnBadArgument,(unsigned long long)observed,
+			 (unsigned long long)rscRanges[i].physical);
+			return false;
+		}
+		if (length<rscRanges[i].length) {
+			SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=map resource=%s result=%d detail=length-short observed=%#llx required=%#llx\n",
+			 rscRanges[i].name,kIOReturnBadArgument,(unsigned long long)length,
+			 (unsigned long long)rscRanges[i].length);
+			return false;
+		}
+		w.length=length;
+		w.base=w.map->getVirtualAddress();
+		if (!w.base) {
+			SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=map resource=%s result=%d detail=no-virtual-base\n",
+			 rscRanges[i].name,kIOReturnNotReady);
+			return false;
+		}
+		SWEET_LOG_INFO("SWEET RSC: phase=map resource=%s result=0 length=%#llx\n",
+		 rscRanges[i].name,(unsigned long long)length);
+	}
+	return true;
+}
+
+bool
+IOQcomRSCChannel::readIdentity(void)
+{
+	uint32_t id=0, solver=0, config=0;
+	if (!readRegister(kIOQcomRscWindowDrv,0,&id)||
+	    !readRegister(kIOQcomRscWindowDrv,4,&solver)||
+	    !readRegister(kIOQcomRscWindowDrv,12,&config)) {
+		SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=identity result=%d detail=read-failed\n",kIOReturnNotReady);
+		return false;
+	}
+	fIdentity.id=id;
+	fIdentity.solver=solver;
+	fIdentity.config=config;
+	fIdentity.major=(id>>16)&255u;
+	fIdentity.tcsCount=(config>>12)&63u;
+	fIdentity.ncpt=(config>>27)&31u;
+	SWEET_LOG_INFO("SWEET RSC: phase=identity id=%#x major=%u solver=%#x tcs=%u ncpt=%u\n",
+	 id,fIdentity.major,solver,fIdentity.tcsCount,fIdentity.ncpt);
+	/* A decoder word of all ones means the window answered nothing at all (the
+	 * same refusal the display owner uses); a major other than 1/2 is a layout
+	 * this firmware cannot parse. Both are refusals before any second read. */
+	if (fIdentity.major<1||fIdentity.major>kIOQcomRscMaxMajor) {
+		SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=identity result=%d detail=identity-version id=%#x major=%u\n",
+		 kIOReturnBadArgument,id,fIdentity.major);
+		return false;
+	}
+	if (solver==~0u) {
+		SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=identity result=%d detail=identity-solver solver=%#x\n",
+		 kIOReturnNotReady,solver);
+		return false;
+	}
+	if (config==~0u) {
+		SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=identity result=%d detail=identity-config config=%#x\n",
+		 kIOReturnNotReady,config);
+		return false;
+	}
+	if (fIdentity.tcsCount<kIOQcomRscTcsMinCount||fIdentity.tcsCount>kIOQcomRscTcsMaxCount) {
+		SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=identity result=%d detail=tcs-count tcs=%u min=%u max=%u\n",
+		 kIOReturnBadArgument,fIdentity.tcsCount,kIOQcomRscTcsMinCount,kIOQcomRscTcsMaxCount);
+		return false;
+	}
+	if (fIdentity.ncpt<kIOQcomRscMinNcpt||fIdentity.ncpt>kIOQcomRscMaxNcpt) {
+		SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=identity result=%d detail=ncpt ncpt=%u min=%u max=%u\n",
+		 kIOReturnBadArgument,fIdentity.ncpt,kIOQcomRscMinNcpt,kIOQcomRscMaxNcpt);
+		return false;
+	}
+	return true;
+}
+
+bool
+IOQcomRSCChannel::mapCommandDB(void)
+{
+	uint32_t address=0, bytes=0;
+	if (!readRegister(kIOQcomRscWindowLocator,0,&address)||
+	    !readRegister(kIOQcomRscWindowLocator,4,&bytes)) {
+		SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=command-db result=%d detail=locator-read-failed\n",
+		 kIOReturnNotReady);
+		return false;
+	}
+	fIdentity.dbAddress=address;
+	fIdentity.dbBytes=bytes;
+	/* The locator is firmware data, not a mapping hint: every field is checked
+	 * before a descriptor is built from it. The physical range is the DRAM band
+	 * the display owner already accepts, and the length is bounded by the same
+	 * 1 MiB the owner refuses beyond. */
+	if (address<kIOQcomRscDbMinPhysical||(address&3u)||(bytes&3u)||
+	    bytes<kIOQcomRscDbHeaderBytes||bytes>kIOQcomRscDbMaxBytes||
+	    (uint64_t)address+bytes>0x100000000ULL) {
+		SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=command-db result=%d detail=command-db-geometry address=%#x bytes=%u\n",
+		 kIOReturnBadArgument,address,bytes);
+		return false;
+	}
+	/* Read-only descriptor: this mapping is a witness, and a direction is part
+	 * of that promise - it is not "read-only for now". */
+	IOMemoryDescriptor *descriptor=
+	 IOMemoryDescriptor::withPhysicalAddress(address,bytes,kIODirectionIn);
+	if (!descriptor) {
+		SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=command-db result=%d detail=descriptor-absent\n",
+		 kIOReturnNoMemory);
+		return false;
+	}
+	Window &w=fWindow[kIOQcomRscWindowDB];
+	w.map=descriptor->map();
+	descriptor->release();
+	if (!w.map||w.map->getPhysicalAddress()!=address||w.map->getLength()!=bytes) {
+		SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=command-db result=%d detail=command-db-map-mismatch address=%#x bytes=%u\n",
+		 kIOReturnNotReady,address,bytes);
+		return false;
+	}
+	w.physical=address;
+	w.length=bytes;
+	w.base=w.map->getVirtualAddress();
+	if (!w.base) {
+		SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=command-db result=%d detail=no-virtual-base\n",
+		 kIOReturnNotReady);
+		return false;
+	}
+	uint32_t version=0, magic=0, flags=0;
+	if (!readRegister(kIOQcomRscWindowDB,0,&version)||
+	    !readRegister(kIOQcomRscWindowDB,4,&magic)||
+	    !readRegister(kIOQcomRscWindowDB,kIOQcomRscDbHeaderBytes,&flags)) {
+		SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=command-db result=%d detail=header-read-failed\n",
+		 kIOReturnNotReady);
+		return false;
+	}
+	fIdentity.dbVersion=version;
+	fIdentity.dbFlags=flags;
+	if (magic!=kIOQcomRscDbMagic) {
+		SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=command-db result=%d detail=command-db-magic magic=%#x expected=%#x\n",
+		 kIOReturnBadArgument,magic,kIOQcomRscDbMagic);
+		return false;
+	}
+	SWEET_LOG_INFO("SWEET RSC: phase=command-db address=%#x bytes=%u version=%#x flags=%#x detail=read-only-map\n",
+	 address,bytes,version,flags);
+	if (version!=1||(flags&1u)) {
+		/* Same two fields the display owner only warns about: this channel is a
+		 * second reader of one firmware block and it must not be stricter than
+		 * the voter that already accepts the boot. The layout it validated is
+		 * still the compatible one, and the row says which. */
+		SWEET_LOG_WARN("SWEET RSC: phase=command-db detail=version-flags-differ version=%#x flags=%#x; validated compatible layout only\n",
+		 version,flags);
+	}
+	return true;
+}
+
+bool
+IOQcomRSCChannel::observeTCS(void)
+{
+	uint32_t enable=0, status=0;
+	if (!readRegister(kIOQcomRscWindowTCS,kIOQcomRscTcsEnable,&enable)||
+	    !readRegister(kIOQcomRscWindowTCS,kIOQcomRscTcsStatus,&status)) {
+		SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=tcs result=%d detail=read-failed\n",kIOReturnNotReady);
+		return false;
+	}
+	fIdentity.irqEnable=enable;
+	fIdentity.irqStatus=status;
+	/* Observed, not owned: the display owner owns both of these words for this
+	 * boot, and this card must not mask, clear or acknowledge anything. The row
+	 * exists so a ring shows the channel looked at a live owner rather than a
+	 * quiet block. */
+	SWEET_LOG_INFO("SWEET RSC: phase=tcs irq-enable=%#x irq-status=%#x detail=observed-not-owned\n",
+	 enable,status);
+	return true;
+}
+
+bool
+IOQcomRSCChannel::readRegister(unsigned window,uint32_t offset,uint32_t *value)
+{
+	if (!value||window>=kIOQcomRscWindowCount||!fLock) return false;
+	bool ok=false;
+	uint32_t observed=0;
+	IOLockLock(fLock);
+	const Window &w=fWindow[window];
+	if (w.base&&(offset&3u)==0&&(uint64_t)offset+sizeof(uint32_t)<=w.length) {
+		observed=*(volatile uint32_t *)(w.base+offset);
+		__asm__ volatile("dmb oshld" ::: "memory");
+		ok=true;
+		fReads++;
+	} else {
+		fReadFailures++;
+	}
+	IOLockUnlock(fLock);
+	if (!ok) return false;
+	*value=observed;
+	return true;
+}
+
+void
+IOQcomRSCChannel::unmapWindows(void)
+{
+	for (unsigned i=0;i<kIOQcomRscWindowCount;i++) {
+		OSSafeReleaseNULL(fWindow[i].map);
+		fWindow[i].base=0;
+		fWindow[i].length=0;
+	}
+	fMapped=false;
+	fIdentity.valid=false;
+}
+
+bool
+IOQcomRSCChannel::start(IOService *provider)
+{
+	if (!IOService::start(provider)) return false;
+	fIdentity.valid=false;
+	fIntid=0;
+	fReads=0;
+	fReadFailures=0;
+	fSubmitAttempts=0;
+	fLock=IOLockAlloc();
+	if (!fLock) {
+		SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=start result=%d detail=lock-absent\n",kIOReturnNoMemory);
+		return false;
+	}
+	if (!validateProperties(provider)) goto fail;
+	if (!mapWindows(provider)) goto fail;
+	fMapped=true;
+	if (!readIdentity()) goto fail;
+	if (!mapCommandDB()) goto fail;
+	if (!observeTCS()) goto fail;
+	/* Nothing below can write, so this is the whole published surface: an
+	 * identity, a read count, and a channel that refuses to submit. */
+	fIdentity.valid=true;
+	fAccepting=true;
+	setProperty("SweetRSCChannelABI",(unsigned long long)kIOQcomRscAbi,32);
+	setProperty("SweetRSCChannelWrites",0ULL,32);
+	setProperty("SweetRSCChannelSubmitAllowed",false);
+	setProperty("SweetRSCChannelIRQ",(unsigned long long)fIntid,32);
+	registerService();
+	fRegistered=true;
+	SWEET_LOG_INFO("SWEET RSC: phase=start result=0 windows=%u ranges=%u reads=%u writes=0 id=%#x tcs=%u ncpt=%u db=%#x db-size=%u irq=%u submit=not-ready\n",
+	 kIOQcomRscWindowCount,kIOQcomRscRangeCount,fReads,fIdentity.id,
+	 fIdentity.tcsCount,fIdentity.ncpt,fIdentity.dbAddress,fIdentity.dbBytes,fIntid);
+	return true;
+fail:
+	fAccepting=false;
+	unmapWindows();
+	if (fLock) {
+		IOLockFree(fLock);
+		fLock=NULL;
+	}
+	SWEET_LOG_ERROR("SWEET RSC: phase=start result=%d detail=refused reads=%u writes=0\n",
+	 kIOReturnBadArgument,fReads);
+	return false;
+}
+
+IOReturn
+IOQcomRSCChannel::submit(uint64_t client,uint32_t address,uint32_t value,uint64_t *token)
+{
+	/* The token is cleared before anything can fail, so a caller that ignores
+	 * the return value still cannot mistake "no transaction" for "accepted". */
+	if (token) *token=0;
+	__atomic_add_fetch(&fSubmitAttempts,1u,__ATOMIC_RELAXED);
+	if (!fAccepting) {
+		SWEET_LOG_WARN("SWEET RSC: phase=submit result=%d detail=channel-not-accepting client=%#llx\n",
+		 kIOReturnNotReady,(unsigned long long)client);
+		return kIOReturnNotReady;
+	}
+	/* NotReady is the answer this card ships. The address and value are logged
+	 * so a consumer that ignores the refusal is visible in the ring, and the
+	 * writes field restates that nothing followed. */
+	SWEET_LOG_INFO("SWEET RSC: phase=submit result=%d detail=read-only-channel client=%#llx address=%#x value=%#x writes=0\n",
+	 kIOReturnNotReady,(unsigned long long)client,address,value);
+	return kIOReturnNotReady;
+}
+
+void
+IOQcomRSCChannel::stop(IOService *provider)
+{
+	fAccepting=false;
+	SWEET_LOG_INFO("SWEET RSC: phase=stop result=0 reads=%u writes=0 submit-attempts=%u\n",
+	 fReads,(unsigned)__atomic_load_n(&fSubmitAttempts,__ATOMIC_RELAXED));
+	unmapWindows();
+	if (fLock) {
+		IOLockFree(fLock);
+		fLock=NULL;
+	}
+	fRegistered=false;
+	super::stop(provider);
+}
+
+void
+IOQcomRSCChannel::free(void)
+{
+	unmapWindows();
+	if (fLock) {
+		IOLockFree(fLock);
+		fLock=NULL;
+	}
+	super::free();
+}
+#endif /* ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MH2LM */
diff --git a/dxb/xnu/iokit/Kernel/arm/IOQcomRSC.h b/dxb/xnu/iokit/Kernel/arm/IOQcomRSC.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..e712c65
--- /dev/null
+++ b/dxb/xnu/iokit/Kernel/arm/IOQcomRSC.h
@@ -0,0 +1,142 @@
+/* SM8150 apps RSC channel: C3-01, one read-only instance.
+ *
+ * Contract of this candidate, in one place:
+ *  - The channel maps the apps RSC the existing display owner already maps:
+ *    DRV 0x18220000 (0x100), the TCS array 0x18220d00 (0x3000) and the command
+ *    DB locator 0x0c3f000c (8), in that ADT reg order. A fourth window is the
+ *    command DB itself, resolved from the locator exactly as the owner does,
+ *    and mapped read-only. Every resolved physical address and length is
+ *    validated before the first register read, so a tree that describes a
+ *    different RSC, a short window or a missing range fails start instead of
+ *    reading whatever memory happened to be mapped.
+ *  - Read-only by construction. This file has no register store, no GIC
+ *    mapping, no interrupt registration, no workloop and no TCS or IRQ-mask
+ *    write. The completion IRQ is carried as a described resource only:
+ *    the ADT says which line the apps RSC owns, and this card records it
+ *    without claiming it, because a second physical handler would be a second
+ *    owner of the same line in the same boot.
+ *  - Identity is read, not assumed: RSC id/major, solver, TCS count and NCPT
+ *    out of the config word, and the command DB version/flags/magic from the
+ *    locator. A major outside the vendor range, a decoder word of all ones, a
+ *    TCS count outside what a 0x3000 array can hold, NCPT outside 3..16 or a
+ *    command DB magic this platform does not use are refusals with their own
+ *    rows, not degraded reads.
+ *  - submit() is the published entry and this card's answer is NotReady. It
+ *    never queues, never stores and never retries; the count of attempts is
+ *    reported so a ring shows the caller was told no.
+ *  - The display owner keeps writing while this channel observes, so the TCS
+ *    interrupt-enable/status words are read once and reported as
+ *    observed-not-owned. That is the whole read budget: ten loads, zero
+ *    stores, and the ring states both.
+ */
+#ifndef IOQCOM_RSC_H
+#define IOQCOM_RSC_H
+
+#include <IOKit/IOService.h>
+#include <IOKit/IOMemoryDescriptor.h>
+
+#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MH2LM)
+
+/* The three ADT reg ranges are the window ABI; the command DB is the fourth
+ * window and it is resolved, never declared. Keeping the two counts apart is
+ * what lets the map rows and the range check disagree loudly. */
+enum : unsigned { kIOQcomRscRangeCount = 3, kIOQcomRscWindowCount = 4 };
+enum : unsigned {
+	kIOQcomRscWindowDrv = 0,
+	kIOQcomRscWindowTCS = 1,
+	kIOQcomRscWindowLocator = 2,
+	kIOQcomRscWindowDB = 3,
+};
+
+/* Vendor geometry, taken from the apps RSC node the display owner already
+ * validates (drivers/soc/qcom/rpmh-rsc.c + sm8150.dtsi): DRV is one 0x100
+ * page, the TCS array is 0x3000 long with a 0x2a0 stride, and the locator is
+ * two words. An array of 0x3000 bytes holds at most 18 TCS; the vendor's own
+ * trees declare 8..18, so anything outside that is somebody else's node. */
+enum : uint32_t {
+	kIOQcomRscAbi = 1,
+	kIOQcomRscTcsStride = 0x2a0,
+	kIOQcomRscTcsMinCount = 8,
+	kIOQcomRscTcsMaxCount = 0x3000 / kIOQcomRscTcsStride,
+};
+
+/* DRV identity words. The config register packs the TCS count in bits 12..17
+ * and NCPT in bits 27..31; the major version lives in bits 16..23 of the id
+ * word. Pre-v3 RSC (major 1) and v3 (major 2) are the only layouts the vendor
+ * driver accepts, and the vendor declares 3..16 channels per TCS. */
+enum : uint32_t {
+	kIOQcomRscMaxMajor = 2,
+	kIOQcomRscMinNcpt = 3,
+	kIOQcomRscMaxNcpt = 16,
+};
+
+/* Command DB: the locator returns the physical address and byte length; the
+ * block starts with a version word, the platform magic and the flag word whose
+ * bit 0 means "not compatible". The magic is the only field that can be a hard
+ * refusal here - the display owner reads the same three words and merely warns
+ * about version/flags, so a second reader that refused them would reject trees
+ * the boot's own voter accepts. */
+enum : uint32_t {
+	kIOQcomRscDbMagic = 0x0c0330dbu,
+	kIOQcomRscDbHeaderBytes = 144,
+	kIOQcomRscDbMinPhysical = 0x80000000u,
+	kIOQcomRscDbMaxBytes = 0x100000,
+};
+
+/* Completion line: LG's SM8150 DTS gives the apps RSC SPI5, i.e. INTID37,
+ * level high. Nothing here touches the GIC; the number is the description this
+ * card hands to the next one. */
+enum : uint32_t { kIOQcomRscIntid = 37, kIOQcomRscIrqAbi = 1 };
+
+class IOQcomRSCChannel : public IOService {
+	OSDeclareDefaultStructors(IOQcomRSCChannel);
+	struct Window {
+		uint64_t physical;      /* expected resolved PA (ADT range, or locator) */
+		IOByteCount length;     /* declared length, also the bounds limit */
+		IOVirtualAddress base;  /* kernel virtual base, 0 when unmapped */
+		IOMemoryMap *map;       /* owned mapping, released on stop/free */
+	};
+	IOLock *fLock;
+	Window fWindow[kIOQcomRscWindowCount];
+	/* What the channel read, kept as evidence for the ring and for the next
+	 * card. fValid means every refusal below passed; nothing else in this
+	 * object is allowed to imply a writable channel. */
+	struct Identity {
+		uint32_t id, solver, config;
+		uint32_t major, tcsCount, ncpt;
+		uint32_t dbAddress, dbBytes, dbVersion, dbFlags;
+		uint32_t irqEnable, irqStatus;
+		bool valid;
+	} fIdentity;
+	uint32_t fIntid;
+	unsigned fReads, fReadFailures, fSubmitAttempts;
+	bool fMapped, fRegistered, fAccepting;
+	bool validateProperties(IOService *provider);
+	bool mapWindows(IOService *provider);
+	bool readIdentity(void);
+	bool mapCommandDB(void);
+	bool observeTCS(void);
+	bool describeIRQ(IOService *provider);
+	void unmapWindows(void);
+	/* The only register accessor in this file. Bounds and alignment are checked
+	 * against the declared window before the load, the load carries the read
+	 * barrier this SoC needs, and the access runs under the channel lock so a
+	 * late caller cannot observe a half-released mapping. Loads only: there is
+	 * no store counterpart to audit, which is the card's whole point. */
+	bool readRegister(unsigned window, uint32_t offset, uint32_t *value);
+public:
+	bool start(IOService *provider) APPLE_KEXT_OVERRIDE;
+	void stop(IOService *provider) APPLE_KEXT_OVERRIDE;
+	void free(void) APPLE_KEXT_OVERRIDE;
+	/* Published entry. This card answers NotReady with a row that says so; it
+	 * has no queue, no deferred write and no retry to hide the refusal behind.
+	 * The token is cleared on every call so a caller cannot mistake "no
+	 * answer" for "accepted". */
+	IOReturn submit(uint64_t client, uint32_t address, uint32_t value,
+	 uint64_t *token);
+	unsigned channelReads(void) const { return fReads; }
+	bool channelSubmitAllowed(void) const { return false; }
+};
+
+#endif /* ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MH2LM */
+#endif /* IOQCOM_RSC_H */
\ No newline at end of file
